  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{s-1}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡s$ is $s\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-2)$ is  $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133684/assets/ac5fed5129673869c7b9e0da3bd46f224aedbfda11b3a26e507b533d66c4e4e9.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
